WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:05.210
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:13.953
I'm standing in the booth and it's tight
in here because it's only made for

00:00:13.953 --> 00:00:16.860
one but we've squished four of us in.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.368
I have my brother on one side.

00:00:19.368 --> 00:00:22.480
And my twin sister on the other and
my mom is standing behind me.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.070
I'm too short to see what she is doing.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.219
I'm only four years old anyway so
I'm a little bit antsy.

00:00:29.219 --> 00:00:32.088
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I start ducking down beneath

00:00:32.088 --> 00:00:35.877
the curtain to check out the feet and
listen to the voices.

00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:39.777
And I ducked down again and I pop up,
and I ducked down for a third time and

00:00:39.777 --> 00:00:41.470
then there's a bright flash.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.760
Play it cool.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.030
That had nothing to do with me.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:52.906
But sure enough, when we walk out of
the booth someone asks my mom for

00:00:52.906 --> 00:00:54.997
my name and information.

00:00:54.997 --> 00:00:59.577
I have been a political junkie for
as long as I can remember, or

00:00:59.577 --> 00:01:04.263
at least since my star turn in
the local paper back in 1990.

00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:09.880
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was born and raised in New Hampshire,

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.690
home of the first in the nation
presidential primary and

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.980
by middle school, I was writing letters
to the editor on the issues of the day.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.648
In high school, I was running for
everything, student senate which I won.

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:26.825
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Homecoming queen, which I lost.

00:01:26.825 --> 00:01:29.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I was no shrinking violet.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:35.060
And I fast forward four years later and
it's my senior year and

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.035
I'm at Bowdoin College in Brunswick,
Maine.

00:01:38.035 --> 00:01:42.030
it's my spring semester and
I'm in this awesome class, ten students.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:46.130
Five versus five, all geared towards
running a campus-wide election.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.040
And my team knows my history and
they look at me and they say, Val,

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.860
you have to run,
you have to be our candidate.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.050
You would be awesome.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.190
And in that moment, I froze.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:06.920
I was so afraid of putting myself
out there, of being a bad candidate,

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:14.250
of losing, and so
I said no, it's not for me.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.240
I'm more of a behind
the scenes type of person.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.159
I guess that was something I'd
internalized over the last four years.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.880
You do it, Tom, you would be awesome.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.016
You be our candidate.

00:02:27.016 --> 00:02:30.917
And he was awesome, he ran and he won.

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.175
And I fast forward another month later and

00:02:33.175 --> 00:02:37.059
I'm sitting in the area living room
of my college president's house.

00:02:37.059 --> 00:02:40.050
I'm there for
a meeting with his wife, asking for

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:43.070
help to get down to Washington DC
to work for a female senator.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.290
She's a serious person.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:52.005
She went on subsequently to be a cabinet
secretary under President Obama.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:56.212
And I'm staring at this oil painting in
the corner of the room not because I care

00:02:56.212 --> 00:02:59.230
about it, but
because she's just asked me a question.

00:03:01.155 --> 00:03:03.710
Val, would you ever consider running for
public office?

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.746
No, i say, it's not for me.

00:03:07.746 --> 00:03:13.230
I'm more of a behind the scenes type
of person, like a chief of staff.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.240
And she says, how can you say that?

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.369
You care so deeply about a woman
being elected to office.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.864
If not you, who?

00:03:25.864 --> 00:03:28.590
I've been turning that question
over in my mind for some time.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:35.890
And I'm here today to challenge
the paradigm that women like me, and women

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:41.610
like all of you are more behind the scenes
type of people, not candidate material.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:47.030
And to the men in the audience, don't
worry, this is important for you too,

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.770
because we're all better served
when more women run for office and

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.530
we have more diverse representation.

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.520
So what's the problem?

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.200
What's the big deal?

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.350
Well, in 2012,
Congress held a hearing on the very

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.160
thorny issue of reproductive rights and
contraceptives.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.890
And to debate that issue
they brought a panel of

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:12.563
experts to testify before the committee.

00:04:12.563 --> 00:04:15.779
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I don't care where you fall on

00:04:15.779 --> 00:04:19.910
the spectrum, this cannot be the best
way to talk about this issue.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.570
We have to do better.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:26.940
But let's level set, where are we?

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.248
We know that women make up
roughly 47% of the workforce.

00:04:32.248 --> 00:04:36.889
And we know that in recent years they
received roughly 57% of college degrees.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:42.714
But when we look across the country,
women make up just 12% of

00:04:42.714 --> 00:04:47.421
governor's offices and
less than 20% of Congress.

00:04:47.421 --> 00:04:51.060
That 20%, by the way, is an all-time high.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.967
And it ties us with an unlikely friend and
ally, Turkmenistan.

00:04:56.967 --> 00:05:01.880
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So how did we get here?

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:09.637
We know that when women run they are as
likely to win as their male counterparts.

00:05:09.637 --> 00:05:14.127
And yet a 2004 Brown study found
that men were 70% more likely

00:05:14.127 --> 00:05:16.740
to indicate a desire to run for office.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.010
And I suppose that's not all that
surprising when you consider

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.600
another study,
that said that a woman has to be asked

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.380
seven times before she
considers running for office.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.095
Most men need to be
encouraged just one time.

00:05:31.095 --> 00:05:33.240
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.625
that encouragement can come from the face
staring back at them in the mirror.

00:05:36.625 --> 00:05:44.590
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I get it.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:46.568
It's hard to run for office.

00:05:46.568 --> 00:05:49.609
You're putting yourself out there,
you're making yourself vulnerable.

00:05:49.609 --> 00:05:52.582
It's messy and there's mudslinging.

00:05:52.582 --> 00:05:56.287
And truthfully, I talked to a US
congressman recently who told me that she

00:05:56.287 --> 00:05:59.994
got nauseous every day on her first
campaign just thinking about going out

00:05:59.994 --> 00:06:02.540
there and
getting herself out in front of voters.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.493
But you are the women of the GSB.

00:06:07.493 --> 00:06:09.400
You've never shied away
from doing something hard.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.860
If not you, who?

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:22.757
So I came to the GSB, and I had dozens
of conversations with my male and

00:06:22.757 --> 00:06:27.167
female classmates, and I said,
I'm going to take it local.

00:06:27.167 --> 00:06:30.009
I've had conversations with many of you
in this room, I think, and I asked,

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:31.694
would you ever consider running for
office?

00:06:33.045 --> 00:06:35.955
And more often than not,
our male classmates said, yeah,

00:06:35.955 --> 00:06:38.615
I'm definitely considering it, 100%.

00:06:38.615 --> 00:06:41.750
Or I haven't considered it,
but I would be great.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:46.595
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And on occasion, hell,

00:06:46.595 --> 00:06:47.885
yes, I should be president.

00:06:47.885 --> 00:06:48.890
What is the hold up?

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:51.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And more often than not,

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:57.226
our female classmate on the other
hand responded with it's not for me.

00:06:57.226 --> 00:07:01.232
I'm more of a behind
the scenes type of person.

00:07:01.232 --> 00:07:06.415
And so I dug a little bit deeper and I did
a survey of close to 300 of our classmates

00:07:06.415 --> 00:07:11.400
and I asked the same question, would
you ever consider running for anything?

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.828
And the response was all too familiar.

00:07:15.828 --> 00:07:20.343
Of our male classmates that responded,
77%,

00:07:20.343 --> 00:07:25.940
all of you said yes, I will run for
office someday, I hope to.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:33.292
And the women, just 44% So
why does it matter?

00:07:35.902 --> 00:07:38.822
These issues that are being
discussed in the political

00:07:38.822 --> 00:07:40.670
arena are incredibly important.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:49.660
We're talking about healthcare,
education, the economy.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.630
These are decisions that are going to
impact to our lives, and

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:55.360
our children's lives, and
our grandchildren's lives.

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:01.350
And you know what, when women run,
they are really good at what they do.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:05.750
Because studies have shown that they are
two times as effective compared to their

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:10.250
male counterparts getting a bill out of
committee and getting that bill enacted.

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:16.500
But when women don't run so
many voices go unheard.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:20.870
So what can we do?

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:24.230
We can run.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:30.220
There are tons of resources out
there to help you be successful,

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:31.880
dozens in California alone.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.938
And I'm standing up here offering
to be a resource for you as well.

00:08:36.938 --> 00:08:41.814
I spent close to five years working
in Congress before I came to the GSB.

00:08:41.814 --> 00:08:43.320
I've been up close and personal.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:48.766
I promise, you are all
qualified to serve in Congress.

00:08:48.766 --> 00:08:53.785
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Trust me.

00:08:53.785 --> 00:08:56.100
And I'm here taking my own advice

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:02.340
because now I can answer yes,
I an considering a run for office.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:06.460
I hope I will have the opportunity to
serve at some point in the future.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:09.660
I've come a long way
from that college senior.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.460
I challenge all of you to do the same.

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:16.930
And today I want you to take
a meaningful step in that direction.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:22.760
If you've counted yourself out,
ask yourself why or why not you.

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:28.050
If you've never considered it,
consider it.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:32.710
And if you're thinking about it at all,
let's talk.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:35.540
I have a really long
history with elections.

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.830
To everybody in the room, including
the men, if you've thought long and

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.810
hard about running for office and
ultimately decided it's not for

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:48.648
you, I hope you will identify,

00:09:48.648 --> 00:09:53.960
encourage, support, and work for
a really good female candidate.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:59.107
You are the student's of the GSB.

00:09:59.107 --> 00:10:03.423
You are the best and the brightest,
the most highly educated,

00:10:03.423 --> 00:10:06.210
the most well connected.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:09.144
You are the future of this country.

00:10:09.144 --> 00:10:12.627
If not you, who?

00:10:12.627 --> 00:10:13.649
Thank you.

00:10:13.649 --> 00:10:16.412
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:16.412 --> 00:10:20.749
[MUSIC]

